($bc2148$conflict-family-in-laws-11$bc2148$,$bc2149$small_gap_both_discontent$bc2149$,$bc2150$Case softened. Both families receive a floor before urgency starts bidding for the rest.$bc2150$,$bc2151$$bc2151$,$bc2152$You agree on the fairness target, yet both feel behind because family time is allocated reactively. The loudest invitation or nearest relative keeps winning. Set minimum connection for each side before adding extras, then keep emergency care separate from social fairness. A baseline prevents every invitation from becoming a fresh referendum on whose family matters.$bc2152$,$bc2153$Choose the minimum seasonal connection your family needs and identify what can be flexible. Choose the minimum for your side and propose the next two dates that protect both baselines.$bc2153$,$bc2154$Case softened. Both families receive a floor before urgency starts bidding for the rest.$bc2154$,$bc2155$Approved v4 template$bc2155$,$bc2156$Rename$bc2156$,$bc2157$$bc2157$,$bc2158$Choose the minimum seasonal connection your family needs and identify what can be flexible.$bc2158$,$bc2159$Choose the minimum for your side and propose the next two dates that protect both baselines.$bc2159$,$bc2160$Both of you save a Two-Family Baseline with minimum contact, prime dates, emergency exceptions, extra-visit rule, and review.$bc2160$,$bc2161$Case softened. Both families receive a floor before urgency starts bidding for the rest.$bc2161$),
($bc2162$conflict-family-in-laws-11$bc2162$,$bc2163$large_gap_both_content$bc2163$,$bc2164$Case softened. Equality protects belonging, and flexibility responds to real life.$bc2164$,$bc2165$[A] wants closer balance; [B] accepts more need-based unevenness.$bc2165$,$bc2166$[A] wants a closer balance because equal visible effort signals that both family bonds belong in the shared life. [B] accepts more unevenness because distance, care needs, or relationship quality makes identical time impractical. You are content because fairness can be defined by minimum connection plus need-based extras. Protect a baseline for each family, then allocate additional time according to current needs without calling the baseline negotiable.$bc2166$,$bc2167$Set the baseline connection your family needs to remain meaningfully included. Define the need-based exceptions and ensure they do not repeatedly consume the other family's baseline.$bc2167$,$bc2168$Case softened. Equality protects belonging, and flexibility responds to real life.$bc2168$,$bc2169$Approved v4 template$bc2169$,$bc2170$Reframe$bc2170$,$bc2171$[A] wants closer balance; [B] accepts more need-based unevenness.$bc2171$,$bc2172$Set the baseline connection your family needs to remain meaningfully included.$bc2172$,$bc2173$Define the need-based exceptions and ensure they do not repeatedly consume the other family's baseline.$bc2173$,$bc2174$Both of you save a Baseline Plus Need plan with family minimums, extra-care criteria, protected dates, and quarterly review.$bc2174$,$bc2175$Case softened. Equality protects belonging, and flexibility responds to real life.$bc2175$),
